HISTORY of BangHash (env2, dumpargs and friends)
================================================

BangHash grew out of one problem: every OS handles the arguments on a
"#!" line differently, so a script like

    #!/usr/bin/php -n -q -d include_path=/foo

works on one system and not on another.  env2 is a replacement for env(1)
that makes it work everywhere.  (The man page, section "A BIT OF HISTORY",
has the background on how kernels and env(1) itself have handled this.)

The original code, 2020, was written by hand by Chris Pollitt.  The 2026
work (marked below) was done with Claude, an AI assistant; the commit
messages from that work carry a Co-Authored-By line.


VERSIONS
--------

  1.0   2020-10-27   first release (git tag 1.0)
  1.1   2020-11-05   "release 1.1" (git tag 1.1)
  1.2   2020-11-12   git tag 1.2
  1.3   2020-11-18   git tag 1.3
  1.4   2026-11-05   version bumped in the source (no git tag)
  2.0   2026-11-06   shellwords splitting becomes the default (no git tag yet)


TIMELINE
--------

2020-10-26 .. 27   The project starts in the CWPutils repository: env2 itself,
                   dumpargs, a sample interpreter, a config-file reader (vars),
                   flag parsing as its own module, a man page, and a test script
                   with expected-output files.  Release 1.0.

2020-10-28         Interim work.  parse_input is renamed split_string.  The flag
                   parser, which had been split out into its own module
                   (parse_flags.cc/.hh), is moved back into env2.cc, in the
                   part that is only built into the env2 program.  Splitting
                   moves to the array utilities.

2020-11-01 .. 05   Fixed-size arrays are replaced by malloc'ed ones.  split_and_
                   merge appears.  usage() and the README are updated.  The code
                   is run through astyle.  A bug in -s is fixed, argv_t is used
                   more, and the config arguments are no longer global.  The
                   sample interpreters are improved.  Backslash handling is
                   fixed.  Release 1.1.

2020-11-06 .. 12   Porting.  Fixes for macOS and Solaris ("Solaris/macOS should
                   now work (in theory)").  configure and config.hh are added
                   (11-07), with KERNEL_SPLIT blocks in the code that detect and
                   handle how the local kernel splits the #! line.  Expected-
                   output files are kept per kernel flavour (t/ks0, t/ks1).  The
                   tests start using real #! lines.  A second debug level.  The
                   -a (Allow) flag and the "~" hints are added, and the man page
                   is cleaned up.  Release 1.2.

2020-11-16         The project moves into the BangHash/ directory of the CWPutils
                   repository, next to BIDeT.

2020-11-18 .. 26   Release 1.3.  A flags_t class replaces simple strings for
                   flag values.  sample_interpreter variation 3, which carried
                   a copy of env2's flag parser (its own comment: "It sucks.
                   Should really copy the way Perl does it."), is removed.  More
                   macOS and Solaris fixes.  t/ks2 is added for a kernel that
                   splits the #! line perfectly (none was ever found).  Cleanup.

2024-10            config_hashbang.pl is added: a Perl tool that checks and
                   rewrites the #! lines of scripts by file extension, and
                   suggests env2 when a line has several interpreter arguments.

2025-05            File mode changes only (49 files marked executable).

2026-11-05         A modernisation pass (with Claude).  See the next section.

2026-11-06         Version 2.0.


THE 2026 WORK
-------------

Features
  * -w: convert the script path to Windows format, like cygpath -w (Cygwin).
  * -v: the help listed it, but only --version worked.  Fixed.
  * parse_switches() (switches.cc): a table-driven switch parser for the
    library, modelled on how Perl handles switches on a #! line (clustered
    letters, several clusters in one string, stop at the first word that is
    not a switch).  env2's own flags use it.  sample_interpreter3 is back as a
    demonstration of using it from a custom interpreter.  The help text is
    generated from the same table, so it cannot drift from the parser.
  * split_shellwords() (shellwords.cc): a port of Perl's shellwords() as a
    plain state machine.  It was checked against Perl itself on every string
    of up to six characters over a small alphabet (about 137,000 strings), and
    the check runs in "make test".
  * Version 2.0 makes shellwords splitting the default.  The original splitter
    is "legacy": -L selects it, and -s, -p and -x (which only mean something to
    it) imply it, so older #! lines behave as before.  -S is still accepted.
    See the COMPATIBILITY section of the man page.

Bugs found and fixed
  * Heap overflows in split_string() and env2() on long arguments or many
    arguments, found with AddressSanitizer (on Linux; Cygwin has none).
    Long script arguments were also silently truncated at 254 characters.
  * A crash on a #! line with no arguments, and basename(NULL) when no
    interpreter was given.
  * An unset HOME was passed to snprintf.
  * The Makefile had a "touch" rule that made make treat env2.cc as a
    disposable intermediate file and delete it.  Replaced by generated header
    dependencies (-MMD).  "make distclean" can now remove config.mk.
  * test_env2.sh used to copy a test config over the real ~/.env2rc and leave it
    there; it now uses a private HOME.
  * exec failures now exit 127 (not found) or 126, like env(1).

Design changes
  * The global flags hash became a plain struct Flags passed by const reference.
  * The identical KERNEL_SPLIT 1 and 2 code paths were merged, then 2 was
    folded into 1 altogether: no kernel with a perfect #! split is known, and
    t/ks2 was a copy of t/ks1.
  * t/fakekernel.pl pretends to be the kernel's #! handler (linux, naive and
    perfect flavours), so each flavour can be tested on any machine.  It drives
    test_configure.sh, which runs configure and env2 against each flavour.
  * The man page was reviewed: typos, the OS notes, BUGS, and a new section
    on quoting and escapes.

What the shellwords comparison found
  The original splitter differs from Perl's shellwords() in four ways: -s keeps
  a backslash in front of whitespace or a quote; -s also strips backslashes inside
  single quotes; empty quotes are dropped (or, with -p, make an extra empty
  argument next to text); and a lone quote is not reported as an error.  These
  were left as they are in the legacy splitter and documented in the man page.
  The tool that finds them is t/splitcheck.cc with t/splitcompare.pl.


THINGS TRIED AND SET ASIDE
--------------------------

  * Flag parsing as a shared module (October 2020), moved back into env2.cc.
    The idea returned, done differently, as parse_switches() in 2026.
  * A copy of env2's parser in sample_interpreter3 (November 2020), removed;
    replaced by parse_switches() in 2026.
  * A kernel flavour that splits the #! line perfectly (KERNEL_SPLIT 2):
    never found, folded into KERNEL_SPLIT 1.
  * getopt_long and boost::program_options for flag parsing: considered,
    judged unsuitable (the kernel hands over one clumped string, and boost is
    heavy for a tool that builds on Solaris and Cygwin).


IDEAS NOT YET DONE
------------------

See the TODO file: environment-variable expansion (${VAR}) in the #! line,
a vector<string> conversion, splitting env2() into phases, a namespace, and
eventually removing the legacy splitter.
